        content: "Even seasoned investors frequently succumb to emotional and structural errors that dismantle SIP compounding:\n\n### Pitfall 1: Pausing or Redeeming During Market Corrections\nThe instinct to halt SIPs when portfolio values show red is the most destructive behavioral mistake. Halting investments during a crash stops the accumulation of discounted units, locking in paper losses and defeating rupee cost averaging.\n\n### Pitfall 2: Portfolio Over-Diversification\nHolding 12 to 15 different equity funds does not provide superior safety; it creates portfolio overlap, excessive fees, and administrative clutter. A core portfolio of 2 to 4 complementary funds (e.g., Nifty 50 Index, Midcap 150 Index, and a global fund) provides comprehensive coverage.\n\n### Pitfall 3: Over-Reliance on Short-Term Past Returns\nChasing the top-performing mutual fund of the preceding 12 months typically leads to buying at cyclical valuation peaks right before mean reversion occurs.\n\n### Pitfall 4: Failing to Match Fund Choice to Time Horizon\nInvesting in high-beta small-cap mutual funds via SIP for a down-payment goal needed in 18 months introduces unacceptable capital volatility.\n\n### Pitfall 5: Ignoring the Systematic Withdrawal Plan (SWP) De-risking Phase\nAn investor who allows their entire corpus to remain 100% in equity until the exact month the money is needed risks a 20% portfolio draw-down on the eve of retirement. De-risking should begin 3 years prior to the goal date by shifting gains into liquid instruments."
